/P 35 0 R
/T (Move to Hold if the share price approaches the target so that the remaining upside is small, or if revised earnings expectations and valuation assumptions imply materially less upside. Become more cautious on \(a\) a renewed rise in NPLs or impairment charges; \(b\) slower NII growth as lower funding costs give less support; \(c\) weaker regional subsidiary performance, currency losses or political disruption; or \(d\) sustainable ROE below the 22% base case, or a higher required return that lowers justified P/B.)
/E (Move to Hold if the share price approaches the target so that the remaining upside is small, or if revised earnings expectations and valuation assumptions imply materially less upside. Become more cautious on \(a\) a renewed rise in NPLs or impairment charges; \(b\) slower NII growth as lower funding costs give less support; \(c\) weaker regional subsidiary performance, currency losses or political disruption; or \(d\) sustainable ROE below the 22% base case, or a higher required return that lowers justified P/B.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [93]
>>
endobj
595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 35 0 R
/T (The cross-checks bracket the target. Peer P/E gives KES 102 but FY25 EPS is flattered by low provisions, so it is treated as an upper bound. Peer P/B gives KES 79 and the dividend-yield method KES 62, but both ignore that Equity earns more on its book and pays out only 30% of profit. The valuation methods are not averaged to determine the target price.)
/E (The cross-checks bracket the target. Peer P/E gives KES 102 but FY25 EPS is flattered by low provisions, so it is treated as an upper bound. Peer P/B gives KES 79 and the dividend-yield method KES 62, but both ignore that Equity earns more on its book and pays out only 30% of profit. The valuation methods are not averaged to determine the target price.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [94]
>>
